{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"At UCSF CAT the S4-300 lane costs about 2.6x the X Plus 25B lane per Gb at every user tier.","detail":"UCSF CAT (page undated, X 100/200-cycle labelled 'Coming Nov. 2024', so ~late 2024 or 2025) lists S4 300-cycle at $5,778 internal / $7,281 external non-profit / $9,101 for-profit, with 2.5B reads per lane (~750 Gb). The 25B 300-cycle lane is $2,850 / $3,591 / $4,448 with 3.25B reads (~975 Gb). Internal: $7.70 per Gb vs $2.92 = 2.6x. External non-profit: $9.71 vs $3.68 = 2.6x. The page does not say whether prices are per lane or per flow cell. I read them as per lane because the 25B price matches other cores' per-lane prices.","url":"https://cat.ucsf.edu/sequencing/sequencing-rates","date":"2024-11 to 2025 (undated)","source_name":"UCSF Center for Advanced Technology sequencing rates","source_type":"price_list","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"UCSF CAT is not among the cores in the exclusion list or the F-items."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"Emory's primate-center core (page updated 11 Dec 2025) charges $20,700 per 6000 S4-300 run and $22,500 per X 25B run, which is about 2.4-2.8x less per read on the X.","detail":"One cost-per-run column covers both platforms. S4 PE150 gives 8-10B reads for $20,700 ($2.07-2.59 per million reads). 25B PE150 gives 26B reads for $22,500 ($0.87 per million). That is 2.4-3.0x per read. The 200-cycle 25B run ($18,600, 26B reads) costs about the same as an S4 200-cycle run ($18,100, 8-10B reads), so the X gives ~2.6-3x the data for the same bill. Single-lane S4 is $4,600. This confirms F228 (Emory lists both platforms) and adds the actual prices and the 2025-26 price level.","url":"https://enprc.emory.edu/nhp_genomics_core/rates/index.html","date":"2025-12-11","source_name":"Emory National Primate Research Center genomics core rates","source_type":"price_list","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"F228 noted only that Emory lists both instruments; the prices and the dated ratio are new."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Core S4-300 flow-cell prices at five cores sit close to Illumina's ~$21.7K list, so the legacy tail is being resold at or above list rather than discounted, while one large institutional account pays about a third less.","detail":"S4-300 flow cell (~3,000 Gb, 4 lanes) versus the $21,683 list (already known): MIT BioMicro FY25 (7/1/2024) $5,400 per lane = $21,600 (-0.4%); Oregon GC3F internal $5,070 per lane = $20,280 (-6%, undated); Calgary 2025 $22,420 (+3%; $21,371 in Jun 2024, +4.9%); UCSF internal $23,112 (+7%); LJI affiliate $23,431 (+8%, undated); Rush $26,460 (+22%, frozen since 2021). Partners/MGB (Mass General Brigham) internal is $14,400 (-34% vs list) with non-MGB academic $20,736 and industry $22,896. Most cores add a margin to reagent cost; only the big internal buyer gets a real discount. The dates mix 2024-26 and some pages are undated.","url":"https://researchcores.partners.org/mogi/services","date":"2024-2026 (mixed, some undated)","source_name":"Partners MOGI, MIT BMC, Oregon GC3F, UCSF CAT, LJI, Calgary CHGI rate pages","source_type":"price_list","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"MGB, LJI, Oregon, UCSF and MIT S4 rows are new. The exclusion list has only Salk/Calgary 6000 prices and F049/F062 (Calgary, UCLA, MD Anderson)."},
{"label":"1B","direction":"supports","claim":"The same 4-lane S4-300 flow cell sells for $14,400 to Mass General Brigham labs but $52,098 on Salk's external list, a 3.6x spread, which shows size and affiliation drive the price.","detail":"Partners MOGI lists the S4-300 4-lane flow cell at $14,400 for MGB labs, $20,736 for non-MGB academic and $22,896 for industry, all per flow cell. The known Salk external non-profit price is $52,098 and Illumina's list is $21,683. MGB's $4.80 per Gb (3,000 Gb) is 34% below list. This gives a real-world marker for how far a large institutional account can sit below list, and the discount applies to a legacy product that is supposedly premium priced. The MGB column is my reading of an internal rate, and the page is undated.","url":"https://researchcores.partners.org/mogi/services","date":"undated (2025-26 likely)","source_name":"Mass General Brigham Research Cores (MOGI)","source_type":"price_list","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"MGB's S4 flow-cell prices are not in the exclusion list; the 34%-below-list marker for a large internal account is new."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Pitt's June 2025 rate sheet shows the 6000 pricing 3.7-4.2x the X on a like-for-like per-Gb basis, and its S4 menu is cut down to one configuration.","detail":"Pitt HTGC (internal rates, effective 6/1/2025): S2 300-cycle full flow cell $14,030 for ~1,250 Gb = ~$11.2 per Gb. X 25B 300-cycle at $21,393 per full flow cell (about 7,500-8,000 Gb) = $2.7-2.9 per Gb, or $2,907 per lane (~$3.0). Ratio = 3.7-4.2x. The fetched S4 rows listed only the 35-cycle option ($14,381); no S4 300-cycle row appeared, and the fetch could be truncated. This is the one core found where the gap approaches or exceeds Illumina's 3.4x. The S2 is a smaller flow cell than the S4, so this exaggerates the 6000 gap, which is why the S4 would give a lower ratio.","url":"https://www.genomics.pitt.edu/about/pricing","date":"2025-06-01","source_name":"University of Pittsburgh Health Sciences Sequencing Core (HTGC)","source_type":"price_list","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Pitt HTGC is not in the exclusion list, and the S2-versus-25B per-Gb ratio is new."},
